<title>Physics XI Unit 7: Gravitation — Academic Companion</title>
</head>
<body>
<h1>Unit 7: Gravitation</h1><p class="meta">Physics XI  |  NEB (Grade 11)  |  Phy. 101 &nbsp;•&nbsp; Content area: Mechanics</p><p class="summary">Newton's universal law is the first law of physics that is genuinely universal: the same constant appears for a falling apple and for a galaxy cluster. The unit also contains the sharpest tension between what the law predicts and what we can see.</p><h2>What the model asserts</h2><ol><li>F = G m1 m2 / r^2 with G = 6.67430e-11 m^3 kg^-1 s^-2, a constant fixed exactly in the 2019 SI and equal and opposite between the two bodies.</li><li>g = GM/R^2 = 9.80665 m/s^2 at the surface by definition; the local value varies with latitude and altitude, and in Nepal it is roughly 9.78 to 9.83 m/s^2.</li><li>Gravitational potential energy is U = -GMm/r, and the field satisfies g = -grad V, so field and potential are two views of one object.</li><li>Escape velocity from the surface is v_e = sqrt(2GM/R) = 11.2 km/s for Earth.</li><li>Kepler's three laws follow from the inverse-square law: elliptical orbits with the Sun at a focus, constant areal velocity, and T^2 proportional to a^3.</li><li>Galaxy rotation curves and gravitational lensing by clusters both show more gravitating mass than the visible matter accounts for.</li><li>The shell theorem makes spherical mass distributions tractable: outside a spherically symmetric body the field is exactly that of a point mass at the centre, which is why planetary orbits have closed-form solutions.</li></ol><h2>Conditions, assumptions and limits</h2><ul><li>Newtonian gravity is an excellent approximation for weak fields (GM/rc^2 much less than 1) and slow speeds (v much less than c); near a black-hole horizon and in the early universe it must be replaced by general relativity.</li><li>The shell theorem - treating an extended body as a point - needs spherical symmetry, and it fails for flattened or irregular mass distributions such as a disc galaxy or a mountain.</li><li>The constant-g treatment near the surface is valid only for heights small compared with Earth's radius; at 3000 m the reduction of g is already about 0.1 %.</li><li>Tidal effects, which come from the differential part of the field and scale as r^(-3), govern ocean tides and the Roche limit; the plain 1/r^2 force does not.</li><li>Kepler's laws are exact only for two bodies in an inverse-square field; with three or more bodies the orbits are not closed and no exact solution exists.</li></ul><h2>Exam coverage — 10 NEB topics</h2><ul><li>Newton's law of gravitation</li><li>Gravitational field strength</li><li>Gravitational potential; gravitational potential energy</li><li>Variation in value of 'g' due to altitude and depth</li><li>Centre of mass and centre of gravity</li><li>Motion of a satellite: orbital velocity and time period of the satellite</li><li>Escape velocity</li><li>Potential and kinetic energy of the satellite</li><li>Geostationary satellite</li><li>GPS</li></ul><h2>Common misconceptions — 3 to unlearn</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>“A satellite in a circular orbit has no net force on it, so it must fly off in a straight line.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> The satellite does have a net force: the gravitational pull of the Earth, which is continuously turning its velocity towards the centre. The misconception comes from reading 'no net force' as 'no forces'.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> In a circular orbit the net force is centripetal, F = G M m / r^2 = m v^2 / r, and it is not zero. The satellite keeps moving because no force is needed to maintain its speed; the force only continually changes the direction of the velocity.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>“The value of g is 9.8 m/s^2 everywhere on the Earth, because g is a constant.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> g is a field strength that depends on position: it varies with latitude (because the Earth rotates and because it is an oblate spheroid), with altitude, and with the local geology. The constant 9.80665 m/s^2 is the standard value adopted by convention.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> g = GM/R^2 at the surface of a spherical Earth. Measured g is about 9.78 m/s^2 at the equator and about 9.83 m/s^2 at the poles, decreases with altitude as g' = g (1 - 2h/R), and varies over Nepal by roughly 0.5 per cent between the Terai plains and the high Himalaya.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>“Gravity between two objects is the same as the force of the Earth on a falling stone, so the Earth must be pulling the stone and the stone must be pulling the Earth with the same force.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> The two are equal in magnitude (a third-law pair) but they act on different bodies, and the accelerations are wildly different because the masses are different.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> F = G m1 m2 / r^2 is one interaction; the equal-and-opposite forces act on the two different bodies. The Earth's acceleration is negligible compared with the stone's because the Earth's mass is about 6 x 10^24 times larger, and the acceleration ratio is the inverse of the mass ratio.</p></div><h2>Workbook — 3 questions with answers</h2><div class="item"><p class="status">numerical</p><p><b>Find the escape velocity from the Earth's surface, given g = 9.8 m/s^2 and R = 6.4 x 10^6 m, and compare it with the orbital velocity of a satellite just above the surface.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> Escape velocity v_e = sqrt(2 g R) = sqrt(2 x 9.8 x 6.4 x 10^6) = sqrt(1.254 x 10^8) = 1.12 x 10^4 m/s = 11.2 km/s. For a circular orbit at the surface, v = sqrt(g R) = sqrt(9.8 x 6.4 x 10^6) = 7.9 x 10^3 m/s = 7.9 km/s. The escape speed is sqrt(2) = 1.414 times the orbital speed.</p></div><div class="item"><p class="status">derivation</p><p><b>Show that the value of g at a height h above the Earth's surface (h much smaller than R) is approximately g' = g (1 - 2h/R).</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> At radius R + h the field strength is g' = GM / (R + h)^2. Since g = GM / R^2, we have g' = g R^2 / (R + h)^2 = g (1 + h/R)^-2. For h &lt;&lt; R, expand: (1 + x)^-2 = 1 - 2x + 3x^2 ... with x = h/R, so g' = g (1 - 2h/R + 3(h/R)^2 ...). To first order, g' = g (1 - 2h/R) = g - 2gh/R. The correction is small: at h = 100 km, g falls by about 3 per cent.</p></div><div class="item"><p class="status">reasoning</p><p><b>A geostationary satellite must stay above the same point of the Earth. Explain the two conditions this places on the orbit, and show why the orbit must lie in the equatorial plane.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> First, the orbital period must equal the Earth's sidereal rotation period, 86 164 s (23 h 56 m 4 s), otherwise the satellite drifts westwards relative to the ground. Second, the orbit must be circular, because an elliptical orbit spends part of each revolution far from the Earth and cannot keep a fixed angular position. With a period T, the orbital radius follows from T^2 = 4 pi^2 r^3 / (G M), giving r = 4.23 x 10^7 m, about 42 200 km from the centre, some 35 800 km above the surface. The orbit lies in the equatorial plane because the orbital angular momentum vector is perpendicular to the plane of the orbit, and for the satellite to keep pace with the Earth's rotation its angular momentum must be parallel to the Earth's rotation axis, which lies in the equatorial plane.</p></div><h2>Key numbers</h2><table><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>Newton's constant G (exact)</td><td>6.67430e-11 m^3 kg^-1 s^-2</td></tr><tr><td>Earth's escape velocity</td><td>11.2 km/s</td></tr><tr><td>Sun's escape velocity</td><td>617.7 km/s</td></tr><tr><td>Surface gravity on the Moon</td><td>about 1.62 m/s^2</td></tr></table><h2>Open questions and mysteries</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>What is dark matter made of, and has any candidate particle ever been detected directly?</b></p><p class="status">Open question</p><p>No candidate has been confirmed; the evidence is gravitational and the identity is unknown (NASA).</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>How did the first stars form, and how long before the first one lit up?</b></p><p class="status">Open question</p><p>Dark matter provides the gravitational scaffolding, but the details of the first objects remain an active modelling challenge.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>Can a stable system of planets be configured, and do real systems migrate between configurations?</b></p><p class="status">Frontier</p><p>Planet-planet scattering, the Lidov-Kozai mechanism and post-Newtonian corrections are all active research areas.</p></div><h2>Inquiry prompts</h2><ol><li>Compute the escape velocity from the Moon and compare it with the value needed for a crewed mission from Earth.</li><li>Measure g in your schoolyard with a simple pendulum, estimate the uncertainty, and compare with the predicted local value.</li><li>Derive Kepler's third law from Newton's law of gravitation and check it against the Moon's and Earth's orbital data.</li></ol><h2>Next steps on this platform</h2><ul><li><a href="/class-11-notes/physics/chapters/gravitation">Read the chapter notes: Gravitation</a></li><li><a href="/lab/ph-th-grav">Gravitation theory lab</a></li><li><a href="/lab/ph-3d-gravitation">Gravitation 3D lab</a></li></ul><h2>Further reading</h2><ul><li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/dark-matter/">NASA: Dark Matter</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/preface">OpenStax: University Physics Volume 1</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/13-1-newtons-law-of-universal-gravitation">OpenStax: Newton's Law of Universal Gravitation</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/13-5-keplers-laws-of-planetary-motion">OpenStax: Kepler's Laws of Planetary Motion</a></li></ul><p class="note">Physics XI — Academic Companion — a companion brief, not a replacement for the prescribed NEB text. The open questions describe live research frontiers; they are enrichment, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed text and syllabus for examinable scope.</p>
</body>
